"""Original antimicrobial chapter: current resistance mechanisms and treatment selection."""
from qbank_authoring import question,ref,finalize
AM=ref('IDSA Guidance on Resistant Gram-Negative Infections, July 2026','https://www.idsociety.org/practice-guideline/amr-guidance/')
VA=ref('ASHP/IDSA/PIDS/SIDP Vancomycin Monitoring Consensus','https://www.idsociety.org/practice-guideline/vancomycin/')
SS=ref('Surviving Sepsis Campaign Adult Guidelines, 2026','https://sccm.org/clinical-resources/guidelines/guidelines/surviving-sepsis-campaign-international-guidelines-for-management-of-sepsis-and-septic-shock-2026')
rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))
q('An isolate is reported susceptible to an antibiotic. Which additional factor is essential before selecting it?', 'Whether the drug, dose and route can achieve effective exposure at the actual infection site',[
 ('Susceptibility makes tissue penetration irrelevant','Laboratory activity does not ensure site exposure.'),('The narrowest agent name is always correct regardless of site','An inactive or poorly penetrating agent is not appropriate stewardship.'),('Every susceptible drug is clinically interchangeable','PK/PD, toxicity and evidence differ.')],
 'An antibiogram is one part of a treatment decision', '**Breakpoints assume specified exposure conditions.**',['Dose, infusion strategy and organ function affect target attainment.','CNS, urine, lung and bloodstream infections have different requirements.'], '**Integrate laboratory, patient and site evidence.**',['Confirm infection rather than colonization.','Review source control and expected clinical response.'],[['Laboratory','In-vitro activity'],['Drug','Achievable exposure'],['Patient/site','Clinical suitability']],[AM])
q('A critically ill patient has ESBL-producing E. coli bloodstream infection. Which definitive category is a preferred option when active and suitable?', 'A carbapenem such as meropenem',[
 ('Ceftriaxone despite the ESBL mechanism and resistance evidence','It is not an effective choice in that setting.'),('Nitrofurantoin because the original source may be urinary','It does not treat bloodstream infection.'),('Piperacillin–tazobactam as universally equivalent for every invasive ESBL infection','The evidence and current guidance do not support that blanket equivalence.')],
 'Invasive ESBL infection differs from a localized bladder infection', '**Carbapenems remain central preferred agents for many invasive ESBL infections.**',['Critical illness affects selection within the class.','Susceptible oral options may have a later role after stability and source control.'], '**Use the complete susceptibility and clinical picture.**',['Do not infer efficacy from the source site alone once bacteremia is present.','Monitor exposure and de-escalate through a validated pathway.'],[['Invasive ESBL','Carbapenem often preferred'],['Bladder-only infection','Different options may suffice'],['Step-down','Requires stability and an effective option']],[AM],style='clinical_scenario')
q('An ESBL-producing E. coli causes bladder-localized cystitis and is susceptible to nitrofurantoin. Which principle is appropriate?', 'A suitable narrow urinary agent can be used instead of automatically choosing a carbapenem',[
 ('The ESBL label makes every noncarbapenem drug inactive','Non-beta-lactam susceptibility can remain.'),('Nitrofurantoin is equally suitable if fever and pyelonephritis develop','Its tissue limitations still apply.'),('Every ESBL isolate mandates indefinite IV therapy','Site and severity determine treatment needs.')],
 'A resistance mechanism does not define the entire clinical treatment plan', '**Localized susceptible cystitis can have effective carbapenem-sparing options.**',['TMP-SMX is another option when active and suitable.','Systemic or upper-tract disease needs a different assessment.'], '**Preserve broad agents without compromising efficacy.**',['Check renal function and contraindications.','Reassess for extension if symptoms or fever change.'],[['ESBL label','Mechanism, not universal resistance'],['Localized bladder','Urinary options may suffice'],['Systemic disease','Requires adequate tissue/blood exposure']],[AM])
q('An E. cloacae complex bloodstream isolate initially tests ceftriaxone susceptible. What resistance concern matters?', 'Inducible AmpC expression can lead to resistance during treatment',[
 ('Every initial susceptible result guarantees stability during ceftriaxone exposure','Induction/derepression can change activity.'),('AmpC affects only antifungals','It is a bacterial beta-lactamase mechanism.'),('Cefepime and ceftriaxone have identical stability to AmpC','Cefepime is generally more stable and a weak inducer.')],
 'Some resistance phenotypes can emerge under antibiotic pressure', '**Species-specific AmpC risk influences definitive selection.**',['Cefepime can be suitable when the MIC and exposure criteria are met.','Coexisting resistance and illness severity also matter.'], '**Do not extrapolate one mechanism to every Enterobacterales species.**',['Review susceptibility and local laboratory advice.','Use an active appropriately dosed regimen for the invasive site.'],[['Organism','E. cloacae complex'],['Mechanism','Inducible AmpC'],['Selection','Stable active agent and adequate exposure']],[AM])
q('A susceptible KPC-producing Enterobacterales isolate causes an invasive infection. Which drug combination has a recognized targeted role?', 'Meropenem–vaborbactam',[
 ('Aztreonam alone as reliable protection against every co-produced enzyme','Other beta-lactamases can hydrolyze it.'),('Ceftriaxone alone','It is generally not an appropriate KPC-directed option.'),('Vancomycin','It does not treat Enterobacterales.')],
 'Carbapenemase identity helps select a beta-lactamase inhibitor', '**Vaborbactam inhibits KPC-type serine carbapenemases.**',['Other active newer beta-lactam combinations also have roles.','Susceptibility testing remains necessary.'], '**Do not assume activity against other carbapenemase classes.**',['NDM and OXA-48-like producers need different choices.','Site, prior exposure and available agents influence the final regimen.'],[['KPC','Serine carbapenemase'],['Targeted option','Meropenem–vaborbactam'],['Limit','Not a universal carbapenemase solution']],[AM])
q('Which newly incorporated option is preferred by IDSA 2026 for suitable NDM-producing invasive Enterobacterales infection when susceptible?', 'Aztreonam–avibactam, with cefiderocol also a preferred option',[
 ('Ceftazidime–avibactam alone as reliable NDM coverage','Avibactam does not inhibit NDM and ceftazidime is hydrolyzed.'),('Meropenem–vaborbactam as a universal NDM inhibitor combination','Vaborbactam does not solve metallo-beta-lactamase activity.'),('Ceftriaxone alone','It is not an adequate directed regimen.')],
 'NDM requires a mechanism-aware strategy', '**Aztreonam resists metallo-beta-lactamase hydrolysis, while avibactam protects it from relevant co-produced serine enzymes.**',['Susceptibility and site still matter.','Clinical comparative evidence remains limited.'], '**Use current availability and expert laboratory interpretation.**',['Ceftazidime–avibactam plus aztreonam remains an alternative when the fixed combination is unavailable.','Do not assume every NDM isolate is susceptible to every proposed option.'],[['NDM','Metallo-beta-lactamase'],['2026 option','Aztreonam–avibactam'],['Another preferred option','Susceptible cefiderocol']],[AM])
q('Why can aztreonam plus an avibactam-containing strategy work against some NDM-producing Enterobacterales?', 'Aztreonam avoids NDM hydrolysis, and avibactam inhibits relevant co-produced serine beta-lactamases that would otherwise destroy aztreonam',[
 ('Avibactam directly inhibits every metallo-beta-lactamase','It does not.'),('Aztreonam is resistant to every beta-lactamase ever produced','Co-produced enzymes can hydrolyze it.'),('The combination acts only by inhibiting protein synthesis','Its principal action is a protected cell-wall-active beta-lactam strategy.')],
 'A combination can address two different resistance obstacles', '**The mechanism is complementary protection rather than direct NDM inhibition by avibactam.**',['Other resistance mechanisms can still defeat the approach.','Use appropriate susceptibility methods and dosing.'], '**Do not infer clinical activity from an enzyme name alone.**',['PBP changes and permeability can matter.','Review response and repeat testing when failure occurs.'],[['Aztreonam','Stable to NDM'],['Avibactam','Blocks relevant serine enzymes'],['Remaining limits','Other enzymes, targets and permeability']],[AM])
q('Which option is preferred by IDSA 2026 for susceptible invasive OXA-48-like Enterobacterales infection?', 'Ceftazidime–avibactam',[
 ('Meropenem–vaborbactam as an equivalent OXA-48 inhibitor strategy','Vaborbactam has limited OXA-48-like inhibition.'),('Imipenem–relebactam as universally equivalent','Relebactam also has limited OXA-48-like inhibition.'),('Tigecycline as a universal preferred bloodstream drug','Its low serum exposure limits that role.')],
 'Different serine carbapenemases are not interchangeable targets', '**Avibactam inhibits important OXA-48-like activity.**',['Confirm susceptibility and the full resistance context.','Newer alternatives exist but are selected from site and evidence.'], '**Use organism-mechanism-specific guidance.**',['Do not group KPC and OXA-48 therapies indiscriminately.','Monitor for treatment-emergent resistance if response fails.'],[['OXA-48-like','Ceftazidime–avibactam preferred'],['Vaborbactam/relebactam','Limited enzyme inhibition'],['Decision','Mechanism plus susceptibility and site']],[AM])
q('A difficult-to-treat resistant P. aeruginosa isolate is susceptible to ceftolozane–tazobactam. Which principle is appropriate?', 'Use a susceptible active newer beta-lactam option with appropriate dosing and site assessment',[
 ('Ceftolozane–tazobactam activity can be assumed for every resistant Pseudomonas isolate','Susceptibility varies.'),('All resistant Pseudomonas require colistin regardless of newer active options','This ignores efficacy and toxicity evidence.'),('The CRE carbapenemase label alone determines every Pseudomonas regimen','Resistance biology differs across organisms.')],
 'DTR Pseudomonas needs organism-specific testing', '**Several newer beta-lactam agents may retain activity.**',['Prior exposure can select resistance.','Site-specific dosing and infusion strategies affect exposure.'], '**Avoid unnecessary toxic combination therapy when an effective preferred beta-lactam strategy is established.**',['Use source control and clinical reassessment.','Repeat susceptibility assessment when relapse or failure suggests change.'],[['Organism','P. aeruginosa'],['Drug selection','Demonstrated activity'],['Failure','Reassess resistance and source']],[AM])
q('Which regimen is preferred in IDSA 2026 for many susceptible invasive CRAB infections?', 'Sulbactam–durlobactam with imipenem-cilastatin or meropenem',[
 ('Colistin plus imipenem as the preferred universal regimen','That is not the preferred current approach.'),('Ceftazidime–avibactam alone regardless of the Acinetobacter mechanism','It is not a reliable universal CRAB strategy.'),('Low-dose ampicillin alone','It is inadequate.')],
 'Sulbactam has direct antibacterial action against Acinetobacter', '**Durlobactam protects sulbactam from relevant beta-lactamase activity.**',['A carbapenem companion is suggested for complementary target effects.','Susceptibility and MBL production can change the plan.'], '**Adapt to actual availability and resistance.**',['IDSA 2026 treats high-dose ampicillin–sulbactam combination as a bridge when the preferred agent is not immediately available.','Use local expert guidance for resistant isolates and resource limitations.'],[['Preferred combination','Sulbactam–durlobactam plus carbapenem'],['Role of sulbactam','Direct PBP activity'],['Limits','Resistance and availability']],[AM])
q('Which statement reflects the 2026 IDSA approach to invasive S. maltophilia infection?', 'Cefiderocol monotherapy is preferred in that guidance, while the limited direct clinical-outcome evidence is acknowledged',[
 ('TMP-SMX is the uniquely mandatory monotherapy in every case','The 2026 guidance changed the preference and places TMP-SMX among combination alternatives.'),('Ceftazidime alone is preferred despite intrinsic beta-lactamases','It is not suggested.'),('Every respiratory isolate proves invasive disease needing treatment','Colonization must first be distinguished from infection.')],
 'A current recommendation can change while evidence uncertainty remains explicit', '**The 2026 preference relies substantially on mechanistic and animal-model evidence.**',['Alternative agents and combinations are selected from susceptibility and patient context.','The recommendation should not be presented as a definitive clinical superiority trial result.'], '**First establish that the organism is causing disease.**',['Review host risk and the sampled site.','Use specialist input for severe or resistant infection.'],[['2026 preference','Cefiderocol'],['Evidence limit','Sparse comparative clinical outcomes'],['First decision','Infection versus colonization']],[AM])
q('A resistant organism is isolated from a chronic wound without clinical infection. Which principle is correct?', 'Resistance does not itself create an indication to treat colonization',[
 ('The broader the resistance, the more mandatory antibiotics become in an asymptomatic site','Treatment needs a clinical indication.'),('Every surface swab identifies a deep invasive pathogen','Surface colonizers may not represent deep disease.'),('A positive culture removes the need for examination','Clinical assessment is central.')],
 'Treating a laboratory result can cause harm without benefit', '**Colonization and infection are different biological states.**',['Host and local inflammatory signs guide attribution.','A deep infection may need representative tissue rather than a surface swab.'], '**Use infection-control measures where relevant without unnecessary systemic therapy.**',['Reassess if the clinical state changes.','Document why antibiotics are or are not indicated.'],[['Culture','Organism detected'],['Infection','Clinical disease requiring attribution'],['Resistance','Changes therapy only when therapy is indicated']],[AM])
q('Which PK/PD target is especially important for beta-lactam efficacy?', 'The duration that free drug concentration remains above the MIC',[
 ('Only the highest peak regardless of time','This does not describe the principal beta-lactam target.'),('Only urinary color change','It is unrelated to antimicrobial exposure.'),('The patient’s antibody titer to the drug','It does not determine beta-lactam efficacy.')],
 'Drug classes require different exposure strategies', '**Beta-lactams are predominantly time-dependent agents.**',['Prolonged infusion can improve target attainment in selected serious infections.','A loading dose ensures prompt initial exposure.'], '**Adjust the maintenance plan to clearance and the infection.**',['Critical illness can increase distribution volume or alter renal clearance.','Check stability, compatibility and practical delivery.'],[['Beta-lactam','Time above MIC'],['Loading','Prompt exposure'],['Maintenance','Sustained effective concentrations']],[SS,AM])
q('Which exposure concept is characteristic of aminoglycoside antibacterial activity?', 'Concentration-dependent killing with an important peak/exposure relationship and toxicity-aware monitoring',[
 ('Only time above MIC matters in the same way as every beta-lactam','The class has different PK/PD properties.'),('Higher trough accumulation always improves safety','Persistent elevated exposure can increase toxicity.'),('Serum and renal monitoring are unnecessary','Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity are important.')],
 'Peak activity and accumulation risk must be considered together', '**Aminoglycosides can have a post-antibiotic effect.**',['Dosing strategy depends on the indication and patient.','Renal clearance strongly affects accumulation.'], '**Use validated dosing and monitoring.**',['Do not transfer extended-interval rules blindly to every synergy or special-population regimen.','Review whether a less toxic active option is preferable.'],[['Activity','Concentration dependent'],['Clearance','Renal function dependent'],['Safety','Exposure and toxicity monitoring']],[AM])

